▪ Dings-Howto-Setup-AWS-EC2

This Howto preserves the current AWS Host Setup as historical
Public-Dings-Server Deployment Evidence. It is not the provider-neutral
Deployment Contract.
❲Establish-Three-Stage-Dings-Deployment❳❪300101042.md❫ proposes the Profile
Names ‵Local-Dings-Server‵, ‵Intra-Dings-Server‵ and
‵Public-Dings-Server‵. They are not yet three new Ontology Dings.

The current External Manifestation uses ‵make prod‵ and ‵make prod-restart‵,
which delegate to ‵dings-host server rsync‵ and
‵dings-host server rsync -restart‵. ‵Dings-Config-Server‵ binds the current AWS
Host, remote User and Path and references Certificate, Key and PEM Dings. These
Values are Manifestation Configuration; Secret Payloads are not portable
public Configuration.

The Commands below must not be executed blindly on a new Host. In particular,
the target Deployment does not require Root SSH Login. It uses explicit
least-privilege Service ownership, observable Planning and verified
restart/rollback behavior.

▪▪ Big-Data Server Profiles

The current AWS Deployment can run without a large local Big-Data Disk.
‵dings-host big init‵ builds ‵Big-Data-Combined‵ from available real Payloads
and zero-Byte Stubs. The PHP Delivery Program serves a non-empty local Payload
directly. When a requested Big-Data Link resolves to a zero-Byte Stub, it
redirects the Browser to the configured pCloud ‵filedn.eu‵ Big-Data Endpoint.

The alternative ‵Local Big-Data‵ Profile materializes the Payload Bytes on the
Server and serves them locally. The selected Profile and remote Endpoint are
Deployment Configuration owned by
❲Establish-Three-Stage-Dings-Deployment❳❪300101042.md❫.

▪▪ Private Dings Projection

After the current rsync, ‵make prod‵ invokes
‵dings-host access delete -scope Local‵ on AWS. The Command reads projected
‵*.access‵ Files and removes the matching equal-basename Files from remote
‵Work-Dir‵ and ‵Day‵ when their Scope is ‵Local‵.

The intended Policy retains private Dings on Local-Dings-Server and
Intra-Dings-Server and excludes them from Public-Dings-Server. The current
transfer-then-delete Mechanism is historical operational Evidence. The target
Deployment derives an explicit Stage Access Projection, excludes forbidden
Manifestations before transfer where practical and verifies their absence
before restarting the public Service.

